In class activity - Think Like an AI Designer

1. For the problem identified in previous assignment , Please complete the following
What are the features?
   Cancer type
   Stage at diagnosis
   Age at onset
   Gender
   Number of chemo sessions
   Number of radiation sessions
   Whether the operation was donated or charged

2. What is the label?
   What outcome should the model learn to predict?
      Treatment outcome — Success or Failure

3. What would training data contain?
   It would be past patient records that have all the features above AND the actual outcome
   already known, so like real cases where we already know if the treatment worked or not.

4. Why should testing data be kept separate?
   Because if you test the model on the same data it trained on, it can just kind of "memorize" the answers
   and look way better than it actually is. Keeping test data separate is the only way to actually see if the model can
   handle patients it's never seen before.

5. What could be a false positive?
   Model says a patient is high-risk (predicts Failure) but they actually end up doing fine.
   This wastes resources on extra monitoring/sessions they didn't really need and might scare the
   patient/family for no reason.

6. What could be a false negative?
   Model says a patient will be fine (predicts Success) but their treatment actually fails.
   This is worse because that patient doesn't get flagged for early intervention, which is literally
   the whole point of building this model.

7. Would precision or recall be more important?
   Recall. Missing a patient who's actually high-risk is way worse than flagging someone who turns out okay.
   A false positive just costs some extra resources, but a false negative means someone who needed help didn't get
   flagged in time. So I'd rather the model over-flag a bit than miss people — that's what recall is about.

Explain your reasoning.

8. What types of poor-quality data could affect this model?
   Missing/incomplete staging info for patients referred late from tribal districts or former FATA areas
   Registry data and operations data not matching up/formatted differently
   Not enough cases for rarer cancer types
   Outcomes only tracked in aggregate instead of per patient
   Data being biased toward patients who show up earlier and have cleaner records

9. What could cause this model to overfit?
   Too few cases for rare cancer types compared to how many features I'm using
   Making the model too complicated for how much data there actually is
   Not using a proper test set or cross-validation
   Model latching onto random patterns specific to this hospital/time
   period that wouldn't hold up on new patients


10. What would make you trust—or not trust—the model's predictions?
   I'd trust it more if it performed consistently across different patient groups, not just the ones with cleaner
   data, and if it was tested on data it never saw before. I'd also want the predicted probabilities to actually line
   up with real outcomes, and for a doctor to be able to confirm the flagged risk factors make medical sense. I'd trust it
   less if it did noticeably worse for underserved/late-referred patients (since that's exactly the group with the messiest data),
   if I couldn't tell why it flagged someone, or if it was never tested outside the original data it trained on.







